WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.401 --> 00:00:02.935
-Our first guest tonight
is an Emmy-nominated actor,

00:00:02.935 --> 00:00:04.270
writer, and musician.

00:00:04.270 --> 00:00:06.205
He's currently starring
in "What Happens Later,"

00:00:06.205 --> 00:00:07.740
which is in theaters
November 3rd.

00:00:07.740 --> 00:00:08.741
Let's take a look.

00:00:08.741 --> 00:00:12.444
-Still a music snob.
Oh, geez.

00:00:12.444 --> 00:00:14.481
Remember,
you used to hide my CDs

00:00:14.481 --> 00:00:15.915
when our friends came over?

00:00:15.915 --> 00:00:18.918
Hey, when our friends came over,
you used to hide my CDs.

00:00:18.918 --> 00:00:20.987
-One CD, one time,

00:00:20.987 --> 00:00:22.522
the "Partridge Family
Christmas" album.

00:00:22.522 --> 00:00:24.623
And I did that
to spare you the humiliation.

00:00:24.623 --> 00:00:26.292
-So my taste of music
was humiliating.

00:00:26.292 --> 00:00:27.894
-Oh, yeah.
-Oh, yeah? To who?

00:00:27.894 --> 00:00:29.395
-To you.
-No, not to me.

00:00:29.395 --> 00:00:31.097
To you.
-To me.

00:00:31.097 --> 00:00:33.800
And to you.
-Sheesh.

00:00:33.800 --> 00:00:35.035
-Please welcome back
to the show,

00:00:35.035 --> 00:00:37.236
our friend David Duchovny,
everybody.

00:00:37.236 --> 00:00:40.239
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:40.239 --> 00:00:51.618 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:51.618 --> 00:00:53.786
-Take a seat.
-Thank you.

00:00:53.786 --> 00:00:55.255
-It's lovely to have you here.

00:00:55.255 --> 00:00:56.589
-It's nice to be back, Seth.

00:00:56.589 --> 00:00:59.492
-You get to be in
a romantic comedy with Meg Ryan.

00:00:59.492 --> 00:01:01.261
-I know.
-She directed it as well.

00:01:01.261 --> 00:01:02.895
-I know. I know these things.
-Shot it.

00:01:02.895 --> 00:01:04.363
You shot it?
Yeah. These are not --

00:01:04.363 --> 00:01:06.199
I'm not telling you
anything you don't know.

00:01:06.199 --> 00:01:09.635
-Really I did?
Whew.

00:01:09.635 --> 00:01:11.737
-Good time to be
David Duchovny.

00:01:11.737 --> 00:01:13.606
You shot most of it
in an airport.

00:01:13.606 --> 00:01:15.041
-Yeah. Now it gets worse...
-Wow.

00:01:15.041 --> 00:01:16.075
-As you go on.
-Meg --

00:01:16.075 --> 00:01:17.443
Meg did not do herself
any favors

00:01:17.443 --> 00:01:19.678
because it's not easy
to shoot a movie in an airport.

00:01:19.678 --> 00:01:22.114
-No, because, well,
it was a regional airport,

00:01:22.114 --> 00:01:24.584
so it did shut down
at like 9:00,

00:01:24.584 --> 00:01:26.819
which is when we did
most of our shooting.

00:01:26.819 --> 00:01:29.355
But we also had to shoot
sometimes during the day,

00:01:29.355 --> 00:01:31.391
and there are just
people going

00:01:31.391 --> 00:01:32.992
to and from
wherever they're going.

00:01:32.992 --> 00:01:34.326
-So it's a working airport.

00:01:34.326 --> 00:01:35.528
-It's a working airport.
-It's not an abandoned airport.

00:01:35.528 --> 00:01:37.063
-And I don't know
if you know this,

00:01:37.063 --> 00:01:39.598
but it's illegal to tell anybody
to do anything in an airport.

00:01:39.598 --> 00:01:42.334
I've never tried to do it
before, but like, you can't say,

00:01:42.334 --> 00:01:44.504
Seth, can you just sit there
for a moment

00:01:44.504 --> 00:01:46.139
while David and Meg
shoot this scene

00:01:46.139 --> 00:01:48.174
and you're like, no,
I've got to get to my gate.

00:01:48.174 --> 00:01:49.542
-Interesting.
-Can't do it.

00:01:49.542 --> 00:01:51.711
-So there's no --
There's no crowd control.

00:01:51.711 --> 00:01:52.878
-Zero.

00:01:52.878 --> 00:01:54.313
-And if I want to come over
and be like,

00:01:54.313 --> 00:01:56.348
I'm a big fan of both of you
while you're filming.

00:01:56.348 --> 00:01:58.451
-That's familiar
because that happened.

00:01:58.451 --> 00:01:59.719
[ Laughter ]

00:01:59.719 --> 00:02:02.287
Yeah, we would do these --
It's -- there's like long

00:02:02.287 --> 00:02:04.657
walk-and talk scenes
in this movie like eight pages,

00:02:04.657 --> 00:02:07.193
and we'd just be strolling
through the airport

00:02:07.193 --> 00:02:08.694
and, you know,
people would recognize Meg,

00:02:08.694 --> 00:02:09.895
or they'd recognize me.

00:02:09.895 --> 00:02:12.064
And I remember we came
after like seven pages

00:02:12.064 --> 00:02:15.234
and we were just like, high wire
acting it like we got it.

00:02:15.234 --> 00:02:16.802
We did seven pages.
We're there.

00:02:16.802 --> 00:02:18.704
We're finally at the stop point

00:02:18.704 --> 00:02:20.306
and this guy goes,
"Hey, it's Meg Ryan."

00:02:20.306 --> 00:02:21.841
And I was, like,
I was in the middle of my line.

00:02:21.841 --> 00:02:23.743
I just said,
"That's not helpful."

00:02:23.743 --> 00:02:25.277
[ Laughter ]

00:02:25.277 --> 00:02:26.312
And then I was back here,

00:02:26.312 --> 00:02:27.580
and Meg was like,
"That was amazing."

00:02:27.580 --> 00:02:29.582
You just like...
-[ Laughing ]

00:02:29.582 --> 00:02:30.916
-I thought it was pretty polite,
"That's not helpful."

00:02:30.916 --> 00:02:32.385
-I mean, I do,
it's very polite.

00:02:32.385 --> 00:02:34.987
I also want to give a break
to a traveler

00:02:34.987 --> 00:02:36.755
at a regional airport
in Arkansas.

00:02:36.755 --> 00:02:39.158
I don't blame them for being
a little thrown by the fact

00:02:39.158 --> 00:02:40.826
that Duchovny and Meg Ryan
are coming down the way.

00:02:40.826 --> 00:02:42.795
-No, I -- I --
It just wasn't helpful.

00:02:42.795 --> 00:02:44.196
That's all I'm saying.
-Yeah, you're right.

00:02:44.196 --> 00:02:46.065
You know what? I think it was --
-We're doing a scene.

00:02:46.065 --> 00:02:47.066
-You gave him
a very honest fair...

00:02:47.066 --> 00:02:48.301
-I wasn't saying
"You're an [bleep]."

00:02:48.301 --> 00:02:49.335
I'm just saying
you're not being helpful.

00:02:49.335 --> 00:02:50.903
[ Laughter ]

00:02:50.903 --> 00:02:53.105
-Where do you, like -- What
is a dressing room situation

00:02:53.105 --> 00:02:55.140
when you're shooting
in an airport?

00:02:55.140 --> 00:02:57.610
-I was in a yoga room.

00:02:57.610 --> 00:02:58.844
This was a fancy regional...

00:02:58.844 --> 00:03:00.913
-The Fayetteville airport
has a yoga room?

00:03:00.913 --> 00:03:02.615
-Had a yoga room.

00:03:02.615 --> 00:03:05.051
-Well, now did people were is
the idea that they'll do yoga?

00:03:05.051 --> 00:03:06.886
-I call it a yoga room.
It was a room.

00:03:06.886 --> 00:03:07.887
-Yeah.
-It said --

00:03:07.887 --> 00:03:09.055
[ Laughter ]

00:03:09.055 --> 00:03:10.590
There was nothing else
in there that said yoga.

00:03:10.590 --> 00:03:12.024
-Okay.
-[ Chuckles ]

00:03:12.024 --> 00:03:13.726
But it was called Yoga room.
-Oh, wow.

00:03:13.726 --> 00:03:14.860
-It was my room.

00:03:14.860 --> 00:03:16.462
Nobody came in to do yoga
while I was there.

00:03:16.462 --> 00:03:17.496
-Okay.

00:03:17.496 --> 00:03:18.932
And I didn't do
any yoga in there,

00:03:18.932 --> 00:03:20.566
but it was --
it was sweet, it was.

00:03:20.566 --> 00:03:22.501
There was no gong.
There was no foam roller.

00:03:22.501 --> 00:03:24.270
-But.
-Yeah, otherwise you just --

00:03:24.270 --> 00:03:26.839
There was just
an air of yoga about it.

00:03:26.839 --> 00:03:28.308
-It smelled more of Cinnabon.

00:03:28.308 --> 00:03:30.076
-Okay. Yeah.
-To be honest with you.

00:03:30.076 --> 00:03:31.877
-I mean, I think
almost every room in an airport

00:03:31.877 --> 00:03:33.679
is kind of a Cinnabon room,
if there's any.

00:03:33.679 --> 00:03:35.681
-I prefer that
to rank human sweat.

00:03:35.681 --> 00:03:37.250
-Yeah. That's true.

00:03:37.250 --> 00:03:38.384
Yeah. You know what?
-Anytime.

00:03:38.384 --> 00:03:39.652
-Sold.

00:03:39.652 --> 00:03:41.187
I want to ask you a question.
I know you grew up

00:03:41.187 --> 00:03:43.189
in New York City,
Lower East Side, right?

00:03:43.189 --> 00:03:45.257
-Yeah.
-And -- And you now...

00:03:45.257 --> 00:03:47.393
-Yeah. Sioux Falls.

00:03:47.393 --> 00:03:48.428
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:03:48.428 --> 00:03:50.229
-Sioux Falls?
[ Chuckles ]

00:03:50.229 --> 00:03:52.131
-I grew up in Sioux Falls.

00:03:52.131 --> 00:03:53.466
[ Laughter ]

00:03:53.466 --> 00:03:55.234
-How?
We're about --

00:03:55.234 --> 00:03:56.635
You know it's
trick-or-treating tomorrow.

00:03:56.635 --> 00:03:58.704
What were your -- What was trick
or treating like growing up

00:03:58.704 --> 00:04:00.807
in New York City?

00:04:00.807 --> 00:04:02.741
-Uh, fraught.
-Yeah.

00:04:02.741 --> 00:04:05.644
Was it as bad as I
might think it was in that era?

00:04:05.644 --> 00:04:07.413
-Yeah.
I mean, well, you know,

00:04:07.413 --> 00:04:08.881
you wanted to find
a big building

00:04:08.881 --> 00:04:10.383
because then you could
just get in and get out.

00:04:10.383 --> 00:04:13.519
-Yeah.
-You could do 100 apartments.

00:04:13.519 --> 00:04:17.489
And you'd have your your loot,
and you'd go home for the year.

00:04:17.489 --> 00:04:21.127
But the older kids also knew
that that was going on,

00:04:21.127 --> 00:04:23.729
and they would lay in wait
for the younger kids.

00:04:23.729 --> 00:04:25.098
They were --
Yeah, aww.

00:04:25.098 --> 00:04:26.531
[ Laughter ]

00:04:26.531 --> 00:04:27.866
They were super lazy.

00:04:27.866 --> 00:04:30.002
And they would just let us go
and get all the candy

00:04:30.002 --> 00:04:31.837
and then like, we'll take that.
-So they just --

00:04:31.837 --> 00:04:34.106
You got candy mugged
as a kid in New York City?

00:04:34.106 --> 00:04:35.108
-Sure did.
-Wow.

00:04:35.108 --> 00:04:36.241
-Yeah.

00:04:36.241 --> 00:04:38.478
And one time, you know,
trick-or-treat,

00:04:38.478 --> 00:04:40.846
you know, like, the --
it's trick-or-treat,

00:04:40.846 --> 00:04:42.515
but you never think
of the treat, right?

00:04:42.515 --> 00:04:43.683
Or you never think
of the trick.

00:04:43.683 --> 00:04:45.451
-Yeah, you assume
the treat is implied.

00:04:45.451 --> 00:04:48.053
You forget that you've also
asked for a trick, right?

00:04:48.053 --> 00:04:50.055
-So I remember knocking
on this,

00:04:50.055 --> 00:04:52.324
this door and this old lady
must have been,

00:04:52.324 --> 00:04:54.393
like, 80 years old,
opened it.

00:04:54.393 --> 00:04:56.028
I say she's 80,
but I was like 8,

00:04:56.028 --> 00:04:57.463
so she might have been 35.

00:04:57.463 --> 00:04:59.866
[ Laughter ]

00:04:59.866 --> 00:05:04.903
And -- And then I said,
"Trick-or-treat"

00:05:04.903 --> 00:05:06.072
And she was like,
"Trick."

00:05:06.072 --> 00:05:10.609
And she just turned
and did a somersault.

00:05:10.609 --> 00:05:12.344
And then she was like, "You."

00:05:12.344 --> 00:05:13.946
I was like,
"I'll take my candy."

00:05:13.946 --> 00:05:16.015
It's like, "No, you got to do --
You got to do the trick."

00:05:16.015 --> 00:05:19.118
So then I did a somersault
and she gave me my candy,

00:05:19.118 --> 00:05:20.853
and then I got mugged
for the candy.

00:05:20.853 --> 00:05:21.854
[ Laughter ]

00:05:21.854 --> 00:05:22.988
-You said to those bullies,

00:05:22.988 --> 00:05:23.990
you're like,
"You owe me a somersault."

00:05:23.990 --> 00:05:25.324
-Yeah, let's see
some somersaults.

00:05:25.324 --> 00:05:28.327
-Do you -- have you --
Are you in your apartment now,

00:05:28.327 --> 00:05:31.463
or do people come by your door
like, are you one of the...?

00:05:31.463 --> 00:05:33.332
-Well, they did
when I lived in Newark.

00:05:33.332 --> 00:05:34.433
I don't live in New York
anymore.

00:05:34.433 --> 00:05:35.434
-Gotcha.
-Yeah.

00:05:35.434 --> 00:05:38.070
In LA, no, nobody comes by.
-Nobody.

00:05:38.070 --> 00:05:40.006
-So it's just a regular day.
-A drive by, drive by,

00:05:40.006 --> 00:05:42.108
8-year-old behind the wheel,
"Trick-or-treat."

00:05:42.108 --> 00:05:43.108
[ Laughter ]

00:05:43.108 --> 00:05:44.543
Trick-or-treat.

00:05:44.543 --> 00:05:45.678
-And you just try to throw a
Reese's through the open window?

00:05:45.678 --> 00:05:47.012
Yeah.
[ Laughs ]

00:05:47.012 --> 00:05:49.648
-Sorry.
Sorry.

00:05:49.648 --> 00:05:51.317
-You're going on tour
with your band?

00:05:51.317 --> 00:05:52.585
-Yeah.
-A European tour.

00:05:52.585 --> 00:05:53.919
-Yeah. Yeah.
-This is very exciting.

00:05:53.919 --> 00:05:55.220
-Yeah. It is.

00:05:55.220 --> 00:05:56.656
-Do you -- have you ever done it
before through Europe?

00:05:56.656 --> 00:05:58.924
-Yeah, we've gone through Europe
and Eastern Europe,

00:05:58.924 --> 00:06:01.360
and this is our second time
touring through Europe.

00:06:01.360 --> 00:06:04.596
And you know, I've tried
to get -- no -- no offense

00:06:04.596 --> 00:06:07.299
to your wonderful drummer
today -- but Fred.

00:06:07.299 --> 00:06:08.934
-Yeah. You've tried to
get Fred to drum for you.

00:06:08.934 --> 00:06:10.970
-I can't get any play
out of Fred. Fred is..

00:06:10.970 --> 00:06:12.538
-Really because Fred
says yes to everybody.

00:06:12.538 --> 00:06:15.774
This is shocking.

00:06:15.774 --> 00:06:18.844
He does not -- I mean he is one
of my most talented

00:06:18.844 --> 00:06:20.379
and least discerning friends.

00:06:20.379 --> 00:06:21.580
-Wow.

00:06:21.580 --> 00:06:23.348
I love Fred so much.

00:06:23.348 --> 00:06:27.019
I did a movie with him,
and because he's one person who,

00:06:27.019 --> 00:06:29.421
and you can corroborate
this or not,

00:06:29.421 --> 00:06:31.624
you're not sure if he's making
fun of you all the time.

00:06:31.624 --> 00:06:32.925
-That's true.

00:06:32.925 --> 00:06:33.925
-Yeah.
-But he's not.

00:06:33.925 --> 00:06:35.260
He's a lovely, sweet guy.

00:06:35.260 --> 00:06:36.262
But you have sinking...
-He's a lovely,

00:06:36.262 --> 00:06:37.430
authentic person.

00:06:37.430 --> 00:06:38.697
-But you never fully trust it.
-...suspicion...

00:06:38.697 --> 00:06:40.433
-Yeah.
-...that he's making fun of you.

00:06:40.433 --> 00:06:41.734
And I don't know
how he does that.

00:06:41.734 --> 00:06:43.335
-The other thing you can be
very confident with Fred

00:06:43.335 --> 00:06:45.604
is even if you have the most
wonderful conversation with him,

00:06:45.604 --> 00:06:47.973
he will walk away
having like the best impression

00:06:47.973 --> 00:06:49.508
of you that he's ever had.

00:06:49.508 --> 00:06:51.844
Like he just absorbs a person's
essence and then he can just...

00:06:51.844 --> 00:06:53.178
-I'll have to check that out.

00:06:53.178 --> 00:06:54.447
-Can I ask some questions?

00:06:54.447 --> 00:06:55.614
Because these
are photos of you as --

00:06:55.614 --> 00:06:57.549
You were a front man.

00:06:57.549 --> 00:06:59.751
And these are very --
This is very rock and roll.

00:06:59.751 --> 00:07:03.188
David, I'm very proud of you.
-It's so embarrassing.

00:07:03.188 --> 00:07:04.690
-This I don't know.

00:07:04.690 --> 00:07:06.525
This strikes me
as less rock and roll.

00:07:06.525 --> 00:07:09.628
Because that's you.
You're on the bottom here.

00:07:09.628 --> 00:07:12.832
-Yeah, well, you know,
if you get a good guitar,

00:07:12.832 --> 00:07:14.934
if you're lucky enough to get
a good guitarist in your band,

00:07:14.934 --> 00:07:16.635
you've got to treat them well.
-Yeah.

00:07:16.635 --> 00:07:21.306
-And so I carry him around
the entire tour on my shoulders.

00:07:21.306 --> 00:07:22.575
-I'd do it for you,
too, if you're...

00:07:22.575 --> 00:07:24.844
-Oh, yeah.
And you have some drummers.

00:07:24.844 --> 00:07:26.245
You have some,
you mentioned guitarists,

00:07:26.245 --> 00:07:27.879
but there are some drummers
back there who.

00:07:27.879 --> 00:07:30.883
-My band is so big that I have
Ben Stiller on the drums.

00:07:30.883 --> 00:07:32.151
Yeah.

00:07:32.151 --> 00:07:34.353
-So how did you get
Ben Stiller to drum for you?

00:07:34.353 --> 00:07:35.454
I just asked him.

00:07:35.454 --> 00:07:37.757
-And he -- he said yes,
and Fred said no.

00:07:37.757 --> 00:07:40.159
[ Laughter ]

00:07:40.159 --> 00:07:41.294
-Ben was terrific.

00:07:41.294 --> 00:07:42.694
He came and rehearsed
a little bit.

00:07:42.694 --> 00:07:47.399
And then we were -- It was
for the Tribeca Film Festival,

00:07:47.399 --> 00:07:50.069
and I have my drummer.

00:07:50.069 --> 00:07:55.040
And then at some point I said,
"Oh, my God, this is terrible.

00:07:55.040 --> 00:07:58.077
My drummer has pulled
a hamstring.

00:07:58.077 --> 00:08:00.179
Is there a drummer
in the house?"

00:08:00.179 --> 00:08:02.214
And then people raise their hand
and then like,

00:08:02.214 --> 00:08:04.584
"Oh, this guy, this guy."
And it was -- it was Ben.

00:08:04.584 --> 00:08:06.084
-So there were
other drummers in the house.

00:08:06.084 --> 00:08:07.220
Well, you know,
there's always --

00:08:07.220 --> 00:08:09.154
Is there a drummer
in the house here?

00:08:09.154 --> 00:08:10.690
-Yeah.
-Yeah, yeah.

00:08:10.690 --> 00:08:12.358
[ Laughter ]

00:08:12.358 --> 00:08:16.429
-There is always going to be
a drummer in the house. Yeah.

00:08:16.429 --> 00:08:20.466
-Now, you, you shot
this film in a airport

00:08:20.466 --> 00:08:22.969
and you are also --
You do not care for flying.

00:08:22.969 --> 00:08:24.203
Are you still
a superstitious flier

00:08:24.203 --> 00:08:25.237
or have you gotten over it?

00:08:25.237 --> 00:08:27.072
-No, not so much anymore.
But when --

00:08:27.072 --> 00:08:29.675
When I was younger,
before I was acting, I would.

00:08:29.675 --> 00:08:32.477
I would, if I got on a flight
where there was a famous person,

00:08:32.477 --> 00:08:35.347
-Uh-huh.
I would -- I would be...

00:08:35.347 --> 00:08:37.817
I would think, "Oh, this plane
is not going down," because...

00:08:37.817 --> 00:08:38.884
[ Laughter ]

00:08:38.884 --> 00:08:40.019
...because that would be weird.

00:08:40.019 --> 00:08:42.888
Like, it doesn't happen
all the time.

00:08:42.888 --> 00:08:44.423
-So you feel more confident

00:08:44.423 --> 00:08:46.025
if there's a celebrity
on your plane.

00:08:46.025 --> 00:08:47.793
-Yeah. Tony Danza, like,
this isn't going down.

00:08:47.793 --> 00:08:48.794
Right?

00:08:48.794 --> 00:08:50.029
[ Laughter ]

00:08:50.029 --> 00:08:52.698
-And then when I started
to get famous, I thought,

00:08:52.698 --> 00:08:54.867
"Oh, people are
looking at me like,

00:08:54.867 --> 00:08:56.469
I got to keep this thing up."

00:08:56.469 --> 00:08:59.004
[ Laughter ]

00:08:59.004 --> 00:09:00.373
And I was just imagining, like,

00:09:00.373 --> 00:09:02.007
if the plane started
to have trouble

00:09:02.007 --> 00:09:03.508
and go down,
everybody's like, "It's you.

00:09:03.508 --> 00:09:04.977
You're not famous enough."

00:09:04.977 --> 00:09:06.978
[ Laughter ]

00:09:06.978 --> 00:09:09.648
"Duchovny, you bastard.
You weren't famous enough."

00:09:09.648 --> 00:09:12.017
-You -- You
had a film at Tribeca.

00:09:12.017 --> 00:09:14.453
Was it being a New Yorker
growing up here?

00:09:14.453 --> 00:09:16.355
Is it special to have a movie
premiere out here?

00:09:16.355 --> 00:09:18.123
-It was amazing.
I mean, just walking around

00:09:18.123 --> 00:09:20.826
neighborhoods that I grew up in
and thinking,

00:09:20.826 --> 00:09:23.696
you know, just how life
changes and grows

00:09:23.696 --> 00:09:26.498
and just neighborhoods
that I was a kid in.

00:09:26.498 --> 00:09:28.167
And now I'm doing these things

00:09:28.167 --> 00:09:30.602
and it's just -- it's just
a rich feeling for me.

00:09:30.602 --> 00:09:32.671
-Well, it's very nice.
-Not funny, but rich.

00:09:32.671 --> 00:09:35.040
-Yeah. Not everything
has to be funny.

00:09:35.040 --> 00:09:37.409
David, you're allowed
some richness in your life.

00:09:37.409 --> 00:09:39.044
-I can't.
If I got to keep that plane up,

00:09:39.044 --> 00:09:40.046
I got to be funny.

00:09:40.046 --> 00:09:41.446
[ Laughter ]

00:09:41.446 --> 00:09:44.550
-You still have
your undefeated record.

00:09:44.550 --> 00:09:46.685
So let me check this.
I checked your Wikipedia.

00:09:46.685 --> 00:09:48.721
Not one of your planes
has crashed, is that correct?

00:09:48.721 --> 00:09:49.988
-Yeah. No.
-I'm doing well.

00:09:49.988 --> 00:09:51.757
-Really good.
-Yeah, really well.

00:09:51.757 --> 00:09:53.025
-It's always such
a pleasure to have you here.

00:09:53.025 --> 00:09:54.059
Thank you so much, buddy.
-Thank you.

00:09:54.059 --> 00:09:55.427
-Have a great tour.

00:09:55.427 --> 00:09:56.829
David Duchovny, everybody.

00:09:56.829 --> 00:09:58.764
"What Happens Later"
opens in theaters Friday.

00:09:58.764 --> 00:10:00.900
We'll be right back
with Reneé Rapp.

